{SECT 0 {EXCHG {PARA 263 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }{TEXT 264 1 "\n" }
{TEXT 266 21 "MAPLE NOTES for MA321" }{TEXT 267 1 " " }}{PARA 262 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 265 55 "School of Mathematics, Statistics & Applied Mathema
tics" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 261 "" 0 "" {TEXT 263 
58 "Please proceed by entering return at each > prompt symbol." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 258 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 258 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 1 
"5" }{TEXT 256 44 ". Sequences, Sets and Lists, Linear Algebra " }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 37 "The f
ollowing topics are described: \n" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 "E
xpression sequences" 1 "" "expseq" }{TEXT -1 2 " ," }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "
" {HYPERLNK 17 "seq " 1 "" "seq" }{TEXT -1 2 ",\n" }{HYPERLNK 17 "sets
 and lists" 1 "" "sets" }{TEXT -1 2 " ," }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{HYPERLNK 17 "intersect, union, minus" 1 "" "inter" }{TEXT -1 4 " ,  \+
" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 "subsop" 1 "" "subsop" }{TEXT -1 1 
" " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 264 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 0 "" }}{PARA 260 "" 0 "expseq" {TEXT 268 30 "Expression Sequences i
n Maple " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 292 "An expression sequence is an ordered collection of Maple expre
ssions separated by commas. We have already seen a number of examples \+
of such a structure as the argument of various commands such as solve \+
and diff. Furthermore, the output of some commands is also always an e
xpression sequence.\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 10 "\nExam
ples:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 8 "restart:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 6 "a,b,c;" }
}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6%%\"aG%\"bG%\"cG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "seq1:=%;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#
>%%seq1G6%%\"aG%\"bG%\"cG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
13 "seq1:=seq1,d;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%%seq1G6&%\"aG%
\"bG%\"cG%\"dG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "seq2:=s
in(3*x);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%%seq2G-%$sinG6#,$*&\"\"$
\"\"\"%\"xGF+F+" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "seq2:=
seq2,x,x;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%%seq2G6%-%$sinG6#,$*&\"
\"$\"\"\"%\"xGF,F,F-F-" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 
"diff(seq2);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#,$*&\"\"*\"\"\"-%$sinG
6#,$*&\"\"$F&%\"xGF&F&F&!\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 16 "seq3:=seq1,seq2;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%%seq3G6)%
\"aG%\"bG%\"cG%\"dG-%$sinG6#,$*&\"\"$\"\"\"%\"xGF0F0F1F1" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT 257 27 "seq \+
- generating a sequence" }{TEXT -1 108 ":  A sequence f(m),f(m+1),...f
(n) indexed by an integer i ranging from m=low to n=high can be genera
ted by :" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 25 "> seq(f(i), i=low..high);" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 9 "Example:\n" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "seq(i^2-1,i = 3..7);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 
"6'\"\")\"#:\"#C\"#N\"#[" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 265 "" 0 "sets" {TEXT 258 14 "Sets and Lists" }{TEXT -1 1 " " }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 85 "A use
ful way of collecting together Maple expressions is provided by sets a
nd lists. " }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 259 4 "Sets" }{TEXT -1 266 ": A set is any expression sequence s
urrounded by braces \{\}. There is no significance to the order in whi
ch elements appear in a set. Furthermore, any element may only appear \+
once. We have previously seen sets used as arguments and outputs of va
rious Maple functions." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT 275 0 "" }{TEXT 260 14 "Set operations" }
{TEXT -1 59 ": The following mathematical set operations are available
 :" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 12 
"> A union B;" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 16 "> A intersect B;" }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 12 "> A minus B;" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 70 "being the union, inters
ection and set difference of two sets A and B. " }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 11 "Examples :\n" }}{PARA 
259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "A:=\{1,2,5,7,2,3\}; " }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "
" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"AG<'\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"&\"\"(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "B:=\{-3,2,4,7\};" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"BG<&!\"$\"\"#\"\"%\"\"(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "NullSet:=\{ \};" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 
"6#>%(NullSetG<\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "A un
ion B;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#<)!\"$\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"%
\"\"&\"\"(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "A union Nul
lSet;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#<'\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"&\"\"(
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "A intersect B;" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#<$\"\"#\"\"(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> \+
" 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "A minus B;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#
<%\"\"\"\"\"$\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 
265 "" 0 "" {TEXT 261 5 "Lists" }{TEXT -1 1 " " }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 186 "A list is any expressi
on sequence surrounded by square brackets [ ]. The order in which elem
ents appear is significant for a list and the same element may appear \+
more than once. Example:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "C:=[1,2,5,7,2,1]; " }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"CG7(\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"&\"\"(F'F&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 
"" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 89 "The number of e
lements of a list or set can be found by using the nops command. Examp
les:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 16 "nops(A);nops(C);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"&" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"'" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 76 "The elements of a list \+
or set can be found using square brackets as follows:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 
"" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "A[5];C[1..4];C
[3];" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"\"(" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#7&\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"&\"\"(" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 
20 "6#\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 262 27 "Manipulating lists and sets" }{TEXT -1 118 ": There
 are no list operations analogous to the set operations above. However
, we can convert a sequence into a set. \n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "convert(C,set);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 
"6#<&\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"&\"\"(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }{TEXT 269 70 "We can concatenate (join)
 two sequences into a single list as follows:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 34 "list1:=[3
,4,6,1];list2:=[a,b,c,d];" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%&list1G
7&\"\"$\"\"%\"\"'\"\"\"" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%&list2G7&
%\"aG%\"bG%\"cG%\"dG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 77 
"seq_list1:=op(list1);seq_list2:=op(list2); # op converts a list to a \+
sequence" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%*seq_list1G6&\"\"$\"\"%
\"\"'\"\"\"" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%*seq_list2G6&%\"aG%\"
bG%\"cG%\"dG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 36 "joined_list
s:=[seq_list1,seq_list2];" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%-joined
_listsG7*\"\"$\"\"%\"\"'\"\"\"%\"aG%\"bG%\"cG%\"dG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 189 "We can sub
stitute elements of sets and lists using the subsop command. An exampl
e suffices to show how this is done: to replace the fourth element of \+
the list C by x*z, we use the command:\n" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 2 "C;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#7(\"\"\"\"\"#\"
\"&\"\"(F%F$" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "subsop(4=
x*z,C);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#7(\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"&*&%\"xGF$
%\"zGF$F%F$" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 265 "" 
0 "array" {TEXT 270 6 "Arrays" }{TEXT -1 1 " " }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 145 "Like conventional prog
ramming languages, MAPLE has a data structure for one-, two-, or multi
-dimensional arrays.  An array is defined as follows:" }}{PARA 259 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 35 "> arrayname:=array
(index range(s));" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 134 "where the array elements are not yet assigned.  As an \+
example, the following command creates an array with two rows and thre
e columns:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> \+
" 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "a:=array(1..2,1..3);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"aG-%&arrayG6%;\"\"\"\"\"#;F)\"\"$7\"" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "print(a);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'matrixG6#7$7%&%\"aG6$\"\"\"F+&F)6$F+\"\"#&F)6$F+\"\"
$7%&F)6$F.F+&F)6$F.F.&F)6$F.F1Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 53 "The array elements can then be individually assigned." 
}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 33 
"a[1,1]:=2;a[2,3]:=6;a[1,3]:=-7.5;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#
>&%\"aG6$\"\"\"F'\"\"#" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>&%\"aG6$\"
\"#\"\"$\"\"'" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>&%\"aG6$\"\"\"\"\"$$
!#v!\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "print(a);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'matrixG6#7$7%\"\"#&%\"aG6$\"\"\"F($
!#v!\"\"7%&F*6$F(F,&F*6$F(F(\"\"'Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 
"" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }{TEXT 271 121 "Alter
natively, one can both specify the dimensions of an array and its elem
ents by using a command of the following kind:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "b:=array(
[[1,4],[2,-3],[6,8]]);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"bGK%'mat
rixG6#7%7$\"\"\"\"\"%7$\"\"#!\"$7$\"\"'\"\")Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 266 "" 0 "evalm" {TEXT 272 14 
"Linear Algebra" }{TEXT -1 1 " " }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 103 "Matrix and vector manipulations are st
raightforward with Maple. The following operations are available:" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 28 "matrix addition            +" }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 31 "matrix multiplication    &*    " }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 33 "matrix inverse              ^(-1)" }}
{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 29 "matrix power               ^n" }}{PARA 
259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 27 "scalar multiplication     *" }}{PARA 259 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 32 "identity matrix             &*()" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 212 "Note that \+
the symbol &* is used for matrix multiplication as opposed to ordinary
 scalar multiplication *. To evaluate the resulting matrices found und
er these operations, the command evalm must be used. Examples:" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 48 "r
estart:A:=array([[1,0,-1],[0,1,3],[-1,3,-2]]); " }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"AGK%'matrixG6#7%7%\"\"\"\"\"!!\"\"7%F+F*\"\"$7%F,F.
!\"#Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "v:=ar
ray([1,-2,0]);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#>%\"vGK%'vectorG6#7%
\"\"\"!\"#\"\"!Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 5 "A&*v;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#-%#&*G6$%\"AG%\"vG" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "evalm(%);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 
"" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'vectorG6#7%\"\"\"!\"#!\"(Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "A^(-1);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "6#*&\"\"\"F$%\"AG!\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "A_inverse:=evalm(%);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 
"6#>%*A_inverseGK%'matrixG6#7%7%#\"#6\"#7#\"\"\"\"\"%#!\"\"F,7%F-F-F-7
%F0F-F0Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "ev
alm(A_inverse&*A);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'matrixG6#7%7%
\"\"\"\"\"!F)7%F)F(F)7%F)F)F(Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 0 4 "k*v;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#*&%\"kG\"\"
\"%\"vGF%" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "evalm(%);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'vectorG6#7%%\"kG,$*&\"\"#\"\"\"F'F+
!\"\"\"\"!Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 267 "" 0 "linalg" {TEXT 273 14 "linalg Package" }{TEXT -1 1 " " 
}}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 120 "Ma
ny other linear algebra features such as transpose, determinants, eige
nvalues etc. can be found in the linalg package." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "with(linalg);" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "print(A);" }}{PARA 11 "" 
1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'matrixG6#7%7%\"\"\"\"\"!!\"\"7%F)F(\"\"$7%F*F,!
\"#Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "det(A);
" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#!#7" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "eigenvals(A);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6%\"
\"\"\"\"$!\"%" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "eigenvec
ts(A);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6%7%\"\"\"F$<#K%'vectorG6#7%\"
\"$F$\"\"!Q(pprint06\"7%F*F$<#KF'6#7%F$!\"$!\"#Q(pprint1F-7%!\"%F$<#KF
'6#7%F$F3\"\"&Q(pprint2F-" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }
}{PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 225 "The output is an expression sequence \+
of lists with three elements. The first element is the eigenvalue, the
 second is the multiplicity of eigenvectors with that eigenvalue and t
he third is a set containing the eigenvector(s)." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 259 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "transpose(A);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'matrixG6#7%7%\"\"\"\"\"!!\"\"7%F)F(
\"\"$7%F*F,!\"#Q(pprint06\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
11 "inverse(A);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#K%'matrixG6#7%7%#\"
#6\"#7#\"\"\"\"\"%#!\"\"F*7%F+F+F+7%F.F+F.Q(pprint06\"" }}}}{MARK "55 \+
3 0" 0 }{VIEWOPTS 1 1 0 1 1 1803 1 1 1 1 }{PAGENUMBERS 0 1 2 33 1 1 }
